WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
Today we're back with unintended consequences, Episode 34, for June 19th, 2026.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.560
Friday, let's wrap the week with a story that has a real lesson in it.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.640
In 1996, a primary care physician in southwestern Virginia wrote a first prescription for oxy-contin

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.460
10 milligrams twice daily.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.840
The doctor told the patient the time release formula would control chronic back pain without

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.560
the dependent risks of shorter acting opioids.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.020
Within 18 months, the same doctor had written several hundreds such prescriptions, confident

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.240
that the medical literature supported an addiction rate below 1%.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.080
The patient, however, soon found the tablets losing effect after four hours and began crushing

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.520
them for faster relief.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.520
That pattern repeated in exam rooms across Appalachia and the Midwest.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.480
Purdue Pharma, controlled by the Sackler family, developed oxy-contin to solve a recognized

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.660
clinical problem.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:05.960
Millions of Americans suffered untreated or under-treated chronic non-cancer pain, yet physicians

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:12.180
hesitated to prescribe morphine or other immediate release opioids because of fears of addiction

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.880
and regulatory scrutiny.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.040
The company's chemists created a controlled release oxy-code-on-tablet intended to deliver

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.760
steady analgesia over 12 hours.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.480
The goal was to reduce the peaks and troughs that could reinforce drug-seeking behavior.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.620
At the time, prevailing medical opinion held that patients in pain rarely became addicted.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:41.380
A 1980 letter in the New England Journal of Medicine reporting only four cases of addiction

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.640
among nearly 12,000 hospitalized patients was widely cited as reassurance.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.440
Pain specialists and primary care leaders welcomed a longer-acting option that might

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.040
let patients function at work and at home.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.860
Regulators at the Food and Drug Administration operating under 1990 standards that accepted

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:09.420
company-submitted studies of short duration approved the drug in December 1995 for moderate

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.560
to severe pain.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.820
Purdue launched oxy-contin in 1996 with an unusually large sales force that visited physicians

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.020
offices more than 600,000 times in the first year alone.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:28.140
One might ask whether that volume of contacts could really shift prescribing habits across

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.480
an entire country, yet consider the arithmetic.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:39.540
600,000 visits spread across perhaps 20,000 to 30,000 targeted doctors would average 20 to

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.540
30 calls per physician in a single year, each call delivering the same charts and videos.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.800
Detailers distributed charts and videos stating that addiction occurred in less than 1% of

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:51.220
patients.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:58.100
That language came from the 1980 letter rather than from any long-term trial of the new formulation.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.880
Early sales figures were encouraging.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.840
By 2000, annual prescriptions exceeded 6,000,000 and revenue reached $1.1 billion.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.740
State Medical Boards and the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Healthcare Organizations simultaneously

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:18.100
began emphasizing pain as a fifth vital sign.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.060
This reinforced the message that aggressive treatment was responsible medicine.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.740
A small number of pain specialists and pharmacists raised concerns about diversion and crushing

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:29.520
of the tablets.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.540
Those warnings remained marginal against the prevailing view that under treatment of

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.000
pain was the larger public health problem.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:43.240
One could object that any new drug faces scattered skepticism, but the scale of the detailing

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.840
effort and the institutional weight placed on treating pain as a vital sign made those cautions

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.120
easy to set aside.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:56.660
The 12-hour duration proved shorter for many patients prompting rapid dose escalation that

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:03.120
produce own internal studies had not fully anticipated, because the tablet contained far more

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.140
oxycodone than previous immediate release products, crushing or chewing it delivered a large

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.080
bolus that produced euphoria and physical dependence.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:19.300
Sales representatives continued to repeat the less than 1% figure even after company Pharmacovigilins

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.820
reports documented hundreds of abuse cases by 1999.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.880
As prescriptions multiplied, so did diversion.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.900
Pills moved from medicine cabinets into high school parking lots and later into informal

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.980
pill mills in Florida and Ohio.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:41.460
When state and federal authorities tightened prescribing rules after 2010, many dependent

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.280
patients turned to cheaper heroin and later to elicitly manufactured fentanyl.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:52.900
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention now attributes roughly 500,000 deaths directly

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:58.980
to prescription opioids and their successors between 1999 and 2022.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.100
Total overdose mortality exceeded 1 million when synthetic opioids are included.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.760
Secondary effects included overwhelmed foster care systems in rural counties, declining labor

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:17.660
force participation among prime age men, and a lasting erosion of trust between patients

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:23.460
with legitimate pain and the physicians who now fear regulatory sanction for writing any

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.620
opioid prescription.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:31.740
Purdue Pharma filed for bankruptcy in 2019 after agreeing to settlements exceeding $8 billion.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.480
Members of the Sackler family contributed an additional $6 billion while retaining personal

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.540
immunity from further civil suits.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:47.400
The Food and Drug Administration required abuse deterrent reformulations and most states enacted

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.880
prescription drug monitoring programs.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.660
Medical education shifted toward multimodal pain management and lower starting doses.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.820
Yet many patients with severe chronic pain now report difficulty finding any prescriber willing

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:02.940
to continue therapy.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:09.500
The abrupt reduction in legitimate supply also accelerated the transition to elicit fentanyl.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.880
Illustrating how supply side interventions can produce new, more dangerous markets.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.060
Pain medicine as a specialty continues to grapple with revised guidelines that still lack robust

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.460
long-term data on functional outcomes.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.980
When a single easily measured claim such as an addiction rate below 1% becomes the cornerstone

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:36.000
of both marketing and clinical practice, complex biological and social feedback loops are easily

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:36.540
overlooked.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:42.400
Incentive structures that reward rapid uptake of a new therapy while externalizing the costs

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:48.160
of dependence will reliably discover any gap between trial conditions and real-world

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:48.520
use.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:54.880
Finally, once a treatment becomes embedded in professional norms and institutional metrics,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:00.500
reversing course requires not only new evidence but also repair of the trust that was spent

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.960
in the original rollout.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:08.660
How might current enthusiasm for rapid adoption of weight loss drugs or AI diagnostic tools

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.160
be stressed tested against similar second order effects?

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:14.040
We'll leave it there for today.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.480
Thanks for thinking through this one with me.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:22.040
If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way and I'll see you tomorrow

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:22.620
with another.

